text:
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "span": "Badarayana holds the view that there is a specific nature (bhava).",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "the gods who have attained their respective stations (padānām) possess the qualification (adhikāra) for the worship of Madhu and others",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "there is a specific nature of radiance (prakāsha-visheshah)",
      "category": "terminology",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"As long as service is rendered to the Supreme Truth (pare tatve), so long is there a specific nature of happiness (sukha-visheshata).\"",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"And because radiance (prakāsha) arises from the Supreme One (parama-eka-mṛte), Hari is to be worshipped.\"",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"the worship of the gods is also prescribed\"",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"All this is to be performed eternally, including all yajñas and other actions.\"",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"the fruits mentioned are not qualified (i.e., they are not subject to specific qualifications or distinctions)\"",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"Although Krishna, the Omniscient, has contemplated only a part (of the truth)\"",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "\"declare that this view does not contradict (the whole)\"",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "critical"
    }
  ]
}
